 //! x86 run-time feature detection is OS independent.
+//!
+//! Fil-C: upstream detects x86 features with the `cpuid` instruction (plus
+//! `xgetbv` for the OS XSAVE state), which rustc emits as *Intel-dialect*
+//! inline assembly (`asm!` defaults to Intel; LLVM records it as
+//! `asm sideeffect inteldialect`).  The Fil-Pizlonator only accepts AT&T
+//! dialect inline assembly, so the upstream code aborted at run time under
+//! Fil-C with "cannot handle inline asm (only AT&T dialect inline assembly
+//! is supported)".  A pure AT&T rewrite is not possible from Rust either:
+//!
+//! * rustc refuses to name `ebx` as an inline-asm operand or clobber on
+//!   x86_64 ("rbx is used internally by LLVM and cannot be used as an
+//!   operand for inline asm"; the register is reserved as LLVM's potential
+//!   base pointer), so `cpuid`'s EBX output can never be declared the way
+//!   the pizlonator's `cpuid` whitelist requires ("cpuid output ebx not
+//!   covered by output constraint or clobber").
+//! * the upstream mov/xchg scratch-register trick (which saves and restores
+//!   `ebx` without declaring it) is rejected for the same reason ("literal
+//!   register %ebx not covered by output constraint or clobber").
+//!
+//! Therefore this implementation detects features by parsing
+//! /proc/cpuinfo instead of executing `cpuid` inline asm.  The Linux kernel
+//! has already merged CPUID, the OS-enabled XCR0 state (via xgetbv at boot)
+//! and CR4.OSXSAVE into the `flags` line, so the result is what the
+//! CPUID-based code computes; for the AVX-512/AMX OS-support checks it is
+//! even more accurate, because the kernel clears the flags when the OS does
+//! not enable the corresponding XCR0 state.  Only the first processor's
+//! `flags` are used: Fil-C builds target x86_64, where the features
+//! `is_x86_feature_detected!` knows about are homogeneous across logical
+//! CPUs; on heterogeneous (hybrid) CPUs this may under-report, which is the
+//! safe direction ("it's safe for us to under-report CPU features, but not
+//! to over-report them").  When /proc/cpuinfo cannot be read, or on targets
+//! without the file-IO feature, no features are reported.
+//!
+//! Consequence: `core::arch::x86::__cpuid`/`__cpuid_count` themselves still
+//! abort at run time under Fil-C (same inline-asm limitation); only
+//! `is_x86_feature_detected!` (and anything built on std_detect) is fixed
+//! by this module.

